= Utility (decision theory) =

== Overview ==
'''Utility (decision theory)''' is a numerical representation of preference or value used in decision theory, game theory, and related formal models.

In modern decision theory, utility commonly represents an ordering over options or outcomes. Higher utility means that the represented preference ranks one option above another under the model.

Ordinal utility preserves only ranking. Cardinal or interval-valued utility carries additional structure that permits meaningful comparisons of differences under specified transformations.

In expected-utility theory, utility values are combined with probabilities. Utility is therefore not simply money, pleasure, happiness, or objective social value, although particular models may choose one of those interpretations.

== Boundaries ==
* utility ≠ money
* utility ≠ happiness by definition
* utility representation ≠ proof that preferences are rational or ethically justified

